mdv: "1.0"
title: Keyboard navigation
---

# Reading a chart without a mouse

The normative requirement is an equivalence: keyboard focus MUST produce the
same readout as hover. Not a similar one, not an abbreviated one — the same,
from the same formatting path, because two paths are two things to keep in
step and one of them will eventually be forgotten.

A chart is **one** tab stop, never one per mark. Arrow keys walk the focus
order from there, Home and End jump to its ends, Page Up and Page Down move
between series, Enter and Space select, Escape drops the readout without
leaving the chart, and `T` toggles the table view. Fifteen data points would
otherwise be fifteen tab stops, and a page of charts would be unusable by
exactly the reader the contract exists for.

```mdv line
title: Response time by service
x: minute
y: [api, worker]
table: true
---
minute,api,worker
0,142,310
1,138,298
2,151,344
3,147,301
4,139,289
```

This case is here to name the contract, not to prove it. Keyboard navigation
is behaviour over time — a key arrives, a readout changes — and a golden is one
document frozen at one instant, so no file in this directory can hold the
evidence. The corpus renders with the interaction layer off on purpose, since
the hit-rect overlay belongs to a live document and would pin nothing but its
own geometry.

So the requirement is declared in `meta.json` rather than derived, which is the
one honest way to record it: the executable evidence is the interaction layer's
own tests, where a synthesised `ArrowRight` and a synthesised pointer move are
asserted to produce the identical readout rows.
